Yes. Since you are using Elastic Cloud (SaaS), the responsibility matrix should reflect the shared responsibility model.

Elastic is responsible for:

Underlying infrastructure (VMs, storage, networking)

Elasticsearch cluster availability

Node provisioning and replacement

Elastic version upgrades (if managed by Elastic Cloud)

Platform patching

Hardware failures

Auto-healing of nodes

Your team is responsible for:

Index lifecycle management (ILM)

Data ingestion (Beats, Elastic Agent, Logstash)

Pipelines and integrations

Kibana dashboards and visualizations

Security (users, roles, API keys)

Alerting and monitoring

Data retention policies

Index templates and mappings

Snapshot policies (if customer-managed)

Capacity planning (choosing deployment size)

For your sheet, I would update the first three rows as follows:

Task ID	Task Description	Detailed Activities	Responsibility
1.1	Cluster & Node Health Monitoring	Monitor deployment health, cluster status, node availability, and raise support cases if required. Underlying infrastructure is managed by Elastic Cloud.	Shared (Elastic / Customer)
1.2	Elastic Version & Platform Maintenance	Elastic Cloud performs platform patching and version maintenance. Customer plans and validates upgrades where applicable.	Elastic
1.3	Infrastructure / Hardware Maintenance	Provisioning, replacement of failed nodes, storage, networking, OS patching, and hardware maintenance.	Elastic
I would also rename "Hardware Deployment and Maintenance" to "Infrastructure & Hardware Maintenance", since in Elastic Cloud you never deploy hardware yourself.

This makes the responsibilities accurate for an Elastic Cloud deployment rather than a self-managed Elasticsearch cluster.


